            <ArticleTitle>Percutaneous coronary intervention as a bridge to renal transplantation in a patient with end-stage renal disease--a case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>415-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Renal transplantation is one of the preferred modes of replacement therapy in patients with end-stage renal disease. Cardiovascular disease remains the leading cause of morbidity and mortality in patients with end-stage renal disease and renal transplant recipients. The authors describe a patient with end-stage renal disease who developed unstable angina before renal transplantation. Emergent cardiac catheterization and percutaneous coronary intervention served as a bridge to his successful renal transplantation without complications.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
